{"id": "effect-133c2b38-ff14-5c58-a028-7f94dc7d9fd1", "sessionId": "faf4c65c-530f-d0c2-ce2f-22f268cde910", "turnId": "turn-4a2f589cba8a06e3855d1fda8a0be24c", "createdAt": 1792408428439, "updatedAt": 1792408428463, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "effect", "title": "file_system.read_file", "detail": {"toolName": "file_system.read_file", "display": {"summary": "Reading C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\src\\any3dview\\scheduler.py", "content": null, "suffix": "", "verb": "Reading", "message": "C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\src\\any3dview\\scheduler.py", "settledVerb": "Read", "settledMessage": "C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\src\\any3dview\\scheduler.py", "statusText": "Reading file"}, "kind": "file_read", "input": {"filePath": "C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\src\\any3dview\\scheduler.py", "offset": null, "limit": 2000}}, "state": {"status": "completed", "output": {"filePath": "C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\src\\any3dview\\scheduler.py", "content": "\"\"\"Thread-safe handoff of immutable update payloads to a viewer thread.\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom queue import Empty, SimpleQueue\nfrom threading import Lock, get_ident\nfrom typing import Any, Callable, Optional\n\n\nclass ViewerScheduler:\n    \"\"\"Queue callbacks for execution by the thread that owns a viewer.\n\n    An optional notifier hook lets a host queue a wakeup on the owning\n    thread as soon as work arrives, replacing idle polling.  The hook may\n    be invoked from any thread and must be thread-safe; a failing hook\n    never removes the queued item, so a later drain still executes it.\n    The first hook failure is recorded and re-raised by the next drain so\n    a broken wakeup cannot disappear silently.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    __slots__ = (\"_owner_thread\", \"_queue\", \"_closed\", \"_notifier\",\n                 \"_lock\", \"_notifier_error\")\n\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self._owner_thread = get_ident()\n        self._queue: SimpleQueue[tuple[Callable[..., Any], tuple[Any, ...], dict[str, Any]]] = (\n            SimpleQueue()\n        )\n        self._closed = False\n        self._notifier: Optional[Callable[[], None]] = None\n        # Guards the closed flag together with the enqueue it covers, so a\n        # close racing a submit can neither strand accepted work nor drop\n        # a wakeup for work that was already queued.\n        self._lock = Lock()\n        self._notifier_error: Optional[BaseException] = None\n\n    def set_notifier(self, notifier: Optional[Callable[[], None]]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Attach or detach the wakeup hook (owner thread setup only).\"\"\"\n\n        self._notifier = notifier\n\n    def _notify(self) -> None:\n        notifier = self._notifier\n        if notifier is None:\n            return\n        try:\n            notifier()\n        except Exception as error:\n            # The payload is already queued; a broken wakeup must not\n            # lose it or crash the producing thread.  Record the first\n            # failure so the owner thread observes it on the next drain.\n            if self._notifier_error is None:\n                self._notifier_error = error\n\n    def submit(\n        self,\n        callback: Callable[..., Any],\n        /,\n        *args: Any,\n        **kwargs: Any,\n    ) -> None:\n        with self._lock:\n            if self._closed:\n                raise RuntimeError(\"viewer scheduler has been closed\")\n            if not callable(callback):\n                raise TypeError(\"callback must be callable\")\n            self._queue.put((callback, args, kwargs))\n        self._notify()\n\n    def pending(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Report whether queued work remains (approximate across threads).\"\"\"\n\n        return not self._queue.empty()\n\n    def drain(self, limit: int = 1024) -> int:\n        if get_ident() != self._owner_thread:\n            raise RuntimeError(\"viewer updates must be drained on the owning thread\")\n        completed = 0\n        failure: Optional[BaseException] = None\n        while completed < max(1, int(limit)):\n            try:\n                callback, args, kwargs = self._queue.get_nowait()\n            except Empty:\n                break\n            try:\n                callback(*args, **kwargs)\n            except Exception as error:\n                # One failing callback must not strand the work queued\n                # behind it; keep draining and re-raise the first failure\n                # once the batch is complete.\n                if failure is None:\n                    failure = error\n            completed += 1\n        if not self._queue.empty():\n            # The bounded FIFO still holds work; re-queue the wakeup so the\n            # remainder is drained without waiting for a new submission.\n            self._notify()\n        if failure is None:\n            failure, self._notifier_error = self._notifier_error, None\n        if failure is not None:\n            raise failure\n        return completed\n\n    def close(self) -> None:\n        with self._lock:\n            # Detach first so no wakeup can observe the half-closed state.\n            self._notifier = None\n            self._closed = True\n            while True:\n                try:\n                    self._queue.get_nowait()\n                except Empty:\n                    break\n        self._notifier_error = None\n\n\n__all__ = [\"ViewerScheduler\"]\n", "numLines": 117, "startLine": 1, "requestedOffset": null, "requestedLimit": 2000, "totalLines": null, "wasTruncated": false}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Read", "message": "117 lines from C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\src\\any3dview\\scheduler.py", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": ""}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "reasoning-4b5bab66-674f-59db-be13-1c3dd0edddff-0", "sessionId": "faf4c65c-530f-d0c2-ce2f-22f268cde910", "turnId": "turn-4a2f589cba8a06e3855d1fda8a0be24c", "createdAt": 1792408442238, "updatedAt": 1792408442238, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "The scheduler currently continues-on-error. The oracle test requires:\n1. `scheduler.drain()` raises ValueError immediately when first callback fails, and `seen==[]` (later callback NOT executed).\n2. `scheduler.pending()` is True after the failure.\n3. Next `drain()` executes 'later' and returns 1.\n\nSo fix: in drain, on callback exception, notify remaining pending work (via `self._notify()`) in a finally, then re-raise immediately. Per the user: \"Preserve scheduler fail-fast by notifying pending work in finally rather than continuing after callback exception.\"\n\nSo drain should be:\n\n```python\ndef drain(self, limit=1024):\n    if get_ident() != self._owner_thread:\n        raise RuntimeError(...)\n    completed = 0\n    try:\n        while completed < max(1, int(limit)):\n            try:\n                callback, args, kwargs = self._queue.get_nowait()\n            except Empty:\n                break\n            callback(*args, **kwargs)\n            completed += 1\n    finally:\n        if not self._queue.empty():\n            self._notify()\n    if self._notifier_error is not None:\n        error, self._notifier_error = self._notifier_error, None\n        raise error\n    return completed\n```\n\nWait — but the notifier error: if the callback raises AND there's a notifier error... The oracle test: submit(fail); submit(seen.append,'later'); drain raises ValueError; pending() True; drain() returns 1 and seen==['later'].\n\nWith my version: drain pops fail, raises ValueError, finally sees queue non-empty → _notify() (notifier appends to wakes — fine). Then ValueError propagates. pending() True. Next drain: pops 'later', executes, seen==['later'], queue empty, completed=1, returns 1. \n\nBut wait — the notifier error handling: if notifier_error is set and no callback failure, we raise it after draining. But if a callback fails, the callback error propagates immediately and notifier_error stays. Hmm, that's fine — next drain would raise it. Actually careful: in the oracle test, notifier is `lambda: wakes.append(1)` which doesn't fail. Fine.\n\nAlso there's a subtlety: the finally block calls _notify() which could itself raise... _notify catches exceptions internally and records them. OK.\n\nBut also: should the notifier_error be raised before or after draining remaining? The current design drains the whole batch then raises notifier_error. With fail-fast, a callback error propagates immediately. If notifier_error exists but no callback error, we drain the batch then raise notifier_error. That preserves \"notifier errors must not strand accepted work without observable failure.\"\n\nHmm, but one issue: if notifier_error is set and a callback also fails, the callback error is raised and notifier_error remains for next drain. Acceptable.\n\nNow the animation compatibility in retained_viewer.py. Let me read the relevant sections: `_animation_item_compatible`, `_apply_animation_item`, `_show_animation_frame`. Also the normals comparison. Let me grep.", "summary": []}
